  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 PM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nThere is a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5) for severe weather on \nSaturday, August 15th, although the forecast is still somewhat \nuncertain due to potential for two separate rounds of storms. A\nstationary front that has brought multiple rounds of rain and \nstorms over the past few days will lift northward Saturday \nmorning as a warm front, bringing the warm and humid airmass \nback overhead. Synoptically, our northwest flow 'Ring of Fire' \npattern continues with a building ridge over the south central \nUS. CAMs are in solid agreement that an MCS develops in Iowa \ntonight and that this will be the feature to watch into tomorrow\nmorning. This MCS should move east through northern Illinois \nand reach northwest Indiana between 08-11Z Saturday morning. \nDespite the diurnally unfavorable timing, this system should \nmaintain its strength given adequate 0-6 km shear of 35-40 kts. \nGiven a linear storm mode, damaging winds will be the main \nthreat from ~5 to 10 AM EDT Saturday. This system should weaken \nas it pushes east, so I'd expect the best potential for any \nstrong to severe storms to be mainly west of IN-15 in northwest \nand north central Indiana. Another round of strong to severe \nstorms is possible in the afternoon and evening, mainly between \n2-11 PM EDT along and east of I-69 in the Slight Risk area. \nModels vary widely on a solution but 30-40 kts of 0-6km shear, \n100-200 m2/s2 of 0-1 SRH, and a narrow corridor of moderate \ninstability (up to 2000 J/kg SBCAPE) may develop. This would be \na favorable environment for damaging winds and an isolated, \nbrief tornado. The afternoon round is highly conditional \ndepending on what happens in the morning and if there is any \ncloud debris leftover that prevents clearing. It's possible that\nthe morning round may be the main show and that nothing \ndevelops in the afternoon and evening if the atmosphere is \nworked over.\n\nAdditional showers and storms are possible Sunday, although they are \nexpected to be sub-severe. WPC does have our entire forecast area in \na Slight Risk (level 2 of 4) for excessive rainfall on Saturday and \nareas south of US 24 in a Marginal Risk on Sunday. Given that the \nground is so saturated south of US 30, where 6-10\"+ of rain has \nfallen since this past Tuesday, it won't be hard to have scattered \noccurrences of renewed flooding. Far northern Indiana, northwest \nOhio, and far southern Lower Michigan have escaped the worst of the \nrain recently but should repeated rounds of showers and storms track \nover these areas Saturday and again Sunday, some concern for \nflooding could develop. Precipitable water values will once again be \nat or above 2 inches, with the NAM and RAP even suggesting potential \nfor them to be as high as 2.5 inches! With such high moisture \ncontent in the airmass overhead (99th percentile PWATs), any storm \ncould be efficient rainfall producer with rain rates between 1-2\" \nper hour. Turn Around, Don't Drown if you encounter a flooded \nroadway! In addition, depending on where the heaviest rain falls, \nsome rivers in northeast and east-central Indiana that are currently \nin Action or Minor flood stage may stay steady or see rises this \nweekend.\n\nA welcome break from daily rain and storm chances is ahead!  We \nfinally see a reprieve in the 'Ring of Fire' pattern Monday and \nTuesday as high pressure briefly builds across the Great Lakes for \n36-48 hours. This will allow for the area to dry out, floodwaters to \nrecede, and for river levels to fall. Temperatures will be \nseasonable with highs in the upper 70s to near 80 degrees.\n\nAnother chance for rain and storms comes Wednesday with a cold \nfront, although it may be short lived. Medium and long range \nguidance hints at potential for another dry period by the end of the \nweek into next weekend.",
